course: biology
lesson: 162
title: Sumatra: the smallest tiger, the hairy rhino, the stinking giant flower and the venomous loris
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sumatra is a long island in Indonesia where a tiger, a rhino, an elephant and an orangutan all live in the same forest, and each of them is a smaller kind found nowhere else.
explain: Island animals often become smaller over many generations, perhaps because there is less room and less food, so Sumatra has the smallest living tiger, the smallest rhino and the smallest Asian elephants.
explain: Think of a fire alarm going off in your school: a smell, a noise or a bright colour can be a warning, and Sumatra's animals use all three, from a stinking flower to a loris with venom in its elbows.
explain: Much of the forest has been cleared for palm oil and paper plantations, so many of these animals are now very rare.
fact: The Sumatran tiger is the smallest living tiger and lives only on Sumatra; fewer than about six hundred are thought to be left in the wild.
fact: The Sumatran rhino is the smallest rhino, has a hairy coat and is the only Asian rhino with two horns; fewer than about fifty are thought to survive.
fact: The titan arum makes one of the biggest flower structures in the world, up to about three metres tall, and it stinks of rotting meat or fish to attract insects.
fact: The titan arum blooms only once every few years, and when one blooms in a botanic garden crowds queue to see and smell it.
fact: The sun bear is the smallest bear and has a very long tongue for licking honey and insects; its loose skin lets it twist round and bite a predator that grabs it from behind.
fact: Tarsiers are tiny primates whose each eye is about as big as their brain; they cannot move their eyes, so they turn their heads almost all the way round.
fact: Tarsiers are the only primates that eat only animals, catching insects, spiders and small lizards.
fact: The slow loris is one of the only venomous primates: it licks a gland on its arm and mixes the venom with its spit before it bites.
fact: Videos of tickled pet lorises raising their arms are not cute: the loris is frightened and getting ready to bite.
fact: Draco lizards glide between trees on flaps of skin held out by long ribs, and males flash a bright yellow throat flag to rivals.
fact: Siamangs are big gibbons that blow up a throat sac and sing loud duets every morning; gibbons are the fastest tree-living mammals that do not fly.
fact: In 2017 scientists named the Tapanuli orangutan as a new species, the first new great ape named in nearly a hundred years, and fewer than eight hundred are thought to exist.
fact: Sumatran elephants are the smallest Asian elephants, and some trained elephants help rangers patrol the forest and lead wild elephants away from farms.
fact: Palm oil is in many foods and soaps, so choosing products from better-run plantations can help the forest.
exercise: Which is the smallest living tiger? | The Sumatran tiger | The Indian Bengal tiger | The Siberian or Amur tiger | The white tiger of India
exercise: About how many Sumatran tigers are thought to be left in the wild? | Fewer than about six hundred | About sixty thousand | About six million | None at all
exercise: What is special about the Sumatran rhino's horns? | It is the only Asian rhino with two horns | It has no horns at all | It has five small horns in a row along its nose and forehead | Its horns are made of bone
exercise: About how many Sumatran rhinos survive? | Fewer than about fifty | About five thousand | About half a million | Several million
exercise: How tall can the flower spike of a titan arum grow? | Up to about three metres | Up to about thirty centimetres | Up to about thirty metres | Up to about three centimetres
exercise: Why do crowds queue at a botanic garden when a titan arum blooms? | It blooms only once every few years | It blooms every single morning at dawn | It makes a singing sound when it opens | It is the only flower left in the garden
exercise: What does a sun bear use its very long tongue for? | Licking honey and insects | Catching fish under water | Cleaning its babies' eyes | Tasting the air like a snake
exercise: How does a sun bear's loose skin help it? | It can twist round and bite a predator holding it | It can spread out its arms and glide from tree to tree at night | It keeps it cool in the sun | It lets it swim faster
exercise: Why does a tarsier turn its head right round? | Its eyes are so big it cannot move them | It has no neck bones | It is looking for its tail | It is dizzy from jumping about all night and is trying to stop the spinning
exercise: What do tarsiers eat? | Only animals such as insects and lizards | Only fruit and leaves | Only grass | Only sweet nectar that it sips from the flowers in the treetops
exercise: Where does a slow loris get its venom? | From a gland on its arm that it licks | From its sharp tail | From poisonous berries it stores in its cheeks | From the frogs it rides on
exercise: What does a pet loris raising its arms when tickled really mean? | It is frightened and ready to bite | It is laughing and happy | It wants a hug | It is stretching its arms after a long sleep in the day and wants to wake up
exercise: What holds out the skin wings of a Draco lizard? | Long ribs | Its front legs | Feathers on its sides | Its flat tail
exercise: What do siamangs do every morning? | Sing loud duets with throat sacs puffed up | Dig new burrows | Fly to the coast | Build big nests of mud and leaves high up in the trees for their babies
exercise: What was the Tapanuli orangutan in 2017? | The first new great ape named in nearly a hundred years | The last orangutan left anywhere in the world, found living all alone | A cartoon orangutan | A gorilla living in Sumatra
exercise: Why has so much Sumatran forest been cleared? | For palm oil and paper plantations | For ski resorts | For rice to feed tigers | Because all the trees died in a very cold winter a few years ago
exercise: How do some trained elephants help in Sumatra? | They patrol with rangers and lead wild elephants away from farms | They pull down the old trees so that new plantations can be planted there | They carry palm oil to ships | They guard tigers in cages
test: «saw a video of a loris getting tickled sooo cute, can i get one» What is right? | No, it was scared, and lorises are wild animals with a venomous bite | Yes, lorises love being tickled, which is why they lift their arms up like that | Yes, they make easy pets | No, they only bite on Tuesdays
test: «2 Sumatran rhinos at a mud hole, how many horns» | 4 | 2 | 6 | 8
test: «bet the Sumatran tiger's the biggest tiger, it's in the jungle» What is right? | No, it is the smallest living tiger | Yes, it is the biggest | Yes, jungle tigers are always biggest | No, it is not a real tiger
test: In a cartoon a bear lives on honey, wears a red top and talks to a piglet. What is true of the real honey-loving sun bear? | It is the smallest bear and licks honey and insects with a very long tongue | It eats only honey and nothing else | It lives in a cosy house in a wood in England and eats marmalade sandwiches | It is the biggest bear in the world
test: What would happen to a tarsier if its head could not turn? | It could not look around, because its huge eyes cannot move | It could see behind itself anyway | It would see better | Nothing at all, because tarsiers never need to look around them at all
test: «it stinks like dead fish in here, what is it» at a botanic garden. What is most likely? | A titan arum is blooming | Someone dropped their lunch | A rhino is in the greenhouse | The garden pond has frozen over
test: Why might a tiger, rhino and elephant on an island be smaller than their cousins on big lands? | On islands with less room and food, smaller animals do better over many generations | The island is colder | They drink salt water | Islands are full of giants that hunt and eat all the big animals first
test: «3 siamangs singing, wait no 4, how many throat sacs puffing» | 4 | 3 | 8 | 1
test: A lizard opens yellow flaps and sails from one tree to the next. What is it? | A Draco lizard gliding on skin held by ribs | A flying snake | A baby bird learning to fly | A bat with yellow wings
test: «why should i care what's in my soap, it's just soap» What links soap to Sumatra? | Palm oil in soap may come from plantations that replaced forest | Soap is made from tiger fat, so buying it hurts the last wild tigers | Soap is made of rhino horn | Nothing, soap has no plant oils
test: If all the Sumatran rhinos alive met in one place, about how many would there be? | Fewer than fifty | About ten thousand | About a million or more | Exactly one, kept in a zoo
exam: Identify the principal characteristic distinguishing the Sumatran rhinoceros from the other rhinoceroses of Asia. | It alone among the Asian rhinoceroses possesses two horns | It alone possesses no horn whatsoever | It is the largest of all living rhinoceroses, being taller than an elephant | It is entirely without hair
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the slow loris. | It is among the very few primates in possession of venom | It subsists entirely upon bamboo | It is the largest primate of Sumatra | It derives pleasure from being tickled
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of the tarsier? | It consumes animal prey exclusively, such as insects and small lizards | It consumes fruit and the young leaves of trees exclusively, and never any animal | It consumes the nectar of flowers exclusively | It consumes grasses exclusively
exam: Ascertain the significance attributed to the naming of the Tapanuli orangutan in the year two thousand and seventeen. | It was the first great ape to be described as a new species in nearly a century | It was the first orangutan ever to be observed by scientists in the forests of Sumatra | It was the last orangutan surviving in the wild | It was identified as a species of gorilla
